WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.650
Whether Claude Code writes your code or your weekly report, when did you last stop and look at how it's set up?

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:16.490
That's worth asking, because it gives you so many ways to make it yours: instructions, skills, sub-agents, and hooks.

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:19.750
But a setup grows one fix at a time.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.470
Months later, rules contradict each other, and all of it loads into every session.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.890
It's like a garden: everything keeps growing,

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.830
the weeds fastest.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:35.250
So the art is keeping all of it fresh and relevant.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:43.290
This starts with Claude Code's own checks, then an audit for what they miss, for example three skills doing one job.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:50.150
So in this adventure, you rebuild your setup lean, with hooks for rules with zero exceptions.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.770
Then you run one real task:

00:00:53.420 --> 00:00:58.570
the same work, with fewer corrections, fewer tokens, and checks that hold.
